    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Shot.WhereCredits" xml:space="preserve">The account menu: topping up hides behind “Purchase Venice Credits”</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step1.Button" xml:space="preserve">Open venice.ai</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step1.Text" xml:space="preserve">Open venice.ai and sign up with an email address or a wallet. API access comes with every plan, the free one included, and there is nothing extra to switch on.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step1.Title" xml:space="preserve">Create a Venice.ai account</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step2.Button" xml:space="preserve">Plans and prices</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step2.Text" xml:space="preserve">API requests spend from the Venice account: credits granted by a paid plan, or dollars added to the balance. A hundred credits are one dollar. The free plan grants no credits, so there will be no answers until the account is funded. Venice quotes prices per million tokens: light models cost cents, the strongest ones dollars.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step2.Title" xml:space="preserve">Put money on the account</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step3.Button" xml:space="preserve">Open API Keys</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step3.Limit" xml:space="preserve">The same dialog sets a spending cap - daily, monthly or total. With one in place a mistake in a single conversation cannot eat the whole balance: Venice simply stops answering until the next day or month.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step3.Text" xml:space="preserve">Open the API section on the Venice site - it also shows what is left on the account. Press “Generate New API Key” and leave the type as “Inference Only”: that is enough to work, while “Admin” gives the key needless power over the account. The key is shown in full exactly once - copy it right away. A lost key is not recovered: you delete it and issue a new one.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step3.Title" xml:space="preserve">Create an API key</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step4.Cmd" xml:space="preserve">[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('VENICE_API_KEY', 'your-key-here', 'User')</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step4.CmdHint" xml:space="preserve">If you would rather not let the program keep the key, set an environment variable instead - it is read at startup and never stored:</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step4.Restart" xml:space="preserve">The program has to be restarted after that: environment variables are read once, at startup. A key pasted on the Key &amp; Info page works right away.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step4.Text" xml:space="preserve">Paste the key into Settings - Key &amp; Info. It goes to disk encrypted by Windows, so only your account can read it back. You can keep several keys and switch between them right there.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step4.Title" xml:space="preserve">Hand the key to the program</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step5.Text" xml:space="preserve">Start the app and send any message. If an answer comes back, the key is in place. The line about a missing key means the variable never arrived: check that it was created for the user rather than the system, and that the app has been restarted. And if Venice answers that funds are insufficient while the account does have money, then the spend limit sits on the key itself; it is changed in the same API section.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Step5.Title" xml:space="preserve">Check that it works</system:String>
